{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfxv7dVsvEeQffCwD3Z8m","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I finished all four steps and checked each one by reading it back from Kibana.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' covers the `kibana_sample_data_logs` index, and its time field is `timestamp`. The response code is stored as text, so the panel and the rule use `response.keyword`.\n2. **Dashboard:** I created 'S4-49a54b66 Web logs' with exactly two panels:\n   - \"Requests over time\": a line chart counting requests per time bucket.\n   - \"Requests by response code\": a bar chart counting requests for each `response.keyword` value (top 20).\n3. **Alert rule:** I created 'S4-49a54b66 5xx errors' as an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It checks every minute and fires when more than 10 matching requests arrive within 5 minutes. It matches the pattern `5[0-9][0-9]` on `response.keyword`, which catches every code from 500 to 599 and nothing else. It was enabled when created, so I didn't need to enable it separately.\n4. **Edit and check:** I renamed the response-code panel to \"Responses by code\", and reading the dashboard back shows the new title. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true` with status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":266,"cache_read_input_tokens":21212,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":266},"output_tokens":450,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0da1e72d-e9d9-58d9-b388-fca8d0eba393","uuid":"11ea67b6-5e0e-454a-91f5-ed8ee250b39e","timestamp":"2026-10-12T19:44:42.022Z","request_id":"req_011Cfxv7dGVKNkacckMVtQfK"}
